WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.877
A malevolent spirit lurks
in the shadows of forests

00:00:02.961 --> 00:00:05.588
and the darkest mountains
of the Philippines.

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:07.674
Capable of adopting
physical form,

00:00:07.757 --> 00:00:11.928
the most terrifying a massive
half-human, half-horse hybrid,

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:15.306
the Filipino Tikbalang
thrives on chaos.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.225
Capable of imitating any human

00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:19.477
and cursing
the living with illness,

00:00:19.561 --> 00:00:21.813
this creature's cruel,
high-pitched laugh

00:00:21.896 --> 00:00:23.606
signals horror to come.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.233
In most modern interpretations,

00:00:25.316 --> 00:00:27.902
the Tikbalang is imagined
primarily with equine features,

00:00:27.986 --> 00:00:30.947
but horses aren't native
to the Philippines.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.825
So where do the origins
of this monster come from?

00:00:33.908 --> 00:00:35.285
[adventurous music]

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:38.955
I'm Dr. Emily's Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:42.167
The Tikbalang is, at its core,
a malevolent spirit being.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.626
While usually incorporeal,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.962
it can take on
physical form as an old man,

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:48.173
monkey,
or horse hybrid creature.

00:00:48.256 --> 00:00:51.509
While their shape may change,
their gender rarely does.

00:00:51.593 --> 00:00:53.553
Tikbalangs are
traditionally male,

00:00:53.636 --> 00:00:57.056
and their equine eyes, their
heads and legs mimic a horse,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.351
with their arms and torsos
appearing human-like.

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:03.354
They're very tall
and very thin--

00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:06.357
features made more distinct
by their ungainly see.

00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:08.693
A Tikbalang is
like a Minotaur,

00:01:08.777 --> 00:01:11.738
except instead of a bull's
head, it has a horse's head,

00:01:11.821 --> 00:01:13.865
a humanoid body and
the horse's feet.

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:15.950
It is a creature that
you would encounter

00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:19.329
at the crossroads
in the provinces.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.623
So when you're going home
at night and you're all alone,

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:24.042
you will meet a Tikbalang.

00:01:24.125 --> 00:01:25.668
That's Budjette Tan,

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:28.588
writer and co-creator of
the comic series "Trese,"

00:01:28.671 --> 00:01:31.341
a series based on Filipino
folklore and mythology

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:34.093
that prominently features
Tikbalang characters.

00:01:34.177 --> 00:01:38.223
I can't remember if it was
through stories that I heard

00:01:38.306 --> 00:01:42.811
from relatives, or was it
a movie that I saw as a kid,

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:47.357
where Dolphy, a very popular
actor in the Philippines

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.567
who's known to be
the king of comedy,

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:50.985
appeared as a Tikbalang

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:54.072
in a movie called
"Once Upon a Time,"

00:01:54.155 --> 00:01:58.409
where we saw Dolphy
transform into a Tikbalang

00:01:58.493 --> 00:02:00.036
and there was even this
scene where he like carries

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:03.873
the two kids and they
jump over the moon.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:05.834
I'm not even sure if
I'm remembering right,

00:02:05.917 --> 00:02:08.586
but these are the images
that are stuck in my head

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.714
as far as seeing a Tikbalang
on screen is concerned.

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:14.759
(Dr. Zarka)
Existing in caves,
trees, and bamboo groves

00:02:14.843 --> 00:02:18.263
deep in the forest, this
monster can imitate anyone

00:02:18.346 --> 00:02:20.557
and can turn invisible
at will,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.059
which it uses for one of
its favorite pastimes--

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:25.353
tricking and killing
local travelers.

00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:26.646
Commonly, the Tikbalang

00:02:26.729 --> 00:02:28.815
will lure someone off
their regular course

00:02:28.898 --> 00:02:31.067
before keeping them
trapped and terrified

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.735
in the forest for days.

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:35.822
Take, for example, the legend
of "The Farmer's Son."

00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:37.907
One day, a farmer's
son was hurrying home

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.577
after running an errand
at his uncle's house

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.288
when a stranger approached
him in the forest.

00:02:43.371 --> 00:02:45.707
Although he tried to
ignore the mysterious man,

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:46.833
from the corner of his eye,

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:50.837
he saw that he was very,
very tall and very thin.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.213
The two began a conversation

00:02:52.297 --> 00:02:54.424
as the man kept in
stride with the boy,

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:57.760
his feet making a clumping
sound as they walked.

00:02:57.844 --> 00:03:01.097
The man's long black hair
hung to his bare shoulders,

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.933
only partially covering
his extremely hairy neck,

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:06.102
and his eyes glowed
with blue fire,

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.605
contrasting with
the red glow of the cigar

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:10.064
in his large mouth.

00:03:10.148 --> 00:03:12.442
The stranger coyly pulled
the boy deeper into the woods

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:13.985
and off his path.

00:03:14.068 --> 00:03:16.321
Lost and frightened,
the boy allowed the man

00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:17.780
to take his hand.

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:20.658
When the boy sat down to rest,
the stranger picked him up

00:03:20.742 --> 00:03:23.870
and carried him further
into a dense bamboo forest.

00:03:23.953 --> 00:03:25.371
The mysterious man
then laughed

00:03:25.455 --> 00:03:27.081
a strange,
high-pitched chuckle

00:03:27.165 --> 00:03:29.792
and abandoned the boy
alone in the dark.

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:31.085
The next morning,
his parents were frantic

00:03:31.169 --> 00:03:34.088
that they could not
find their son.

00:03:34.172 --> 00:03:36.883
After a long search, they
found the terrified boy

00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:38.927
sick and delirious with fever.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.888
Falling ill is a common
element of the legend.

00:03:41.971 --> 00:03:44.766
It's said that to even catch
a glimpse of this creature

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:47.435
might cause illness,
a fever or a headache,

00:03:47.518 --> 00:03:49.020
or even blindness.

00:03:49.103 --> 00:03:52.732
Sudden death is also an even
unluckier consequence.

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:54.359
If a Tikbalang
calls out your name,

00:03:54.442 --> 00:03:55.818
wishing illness upon you,

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.862
there is little hope for a cure.

00:03:57.946 --> 00:04:00.698
It was five days before the
boy's fever finally broke

00:04:00.782 --> 00:04:02.784
and he regained consciousness.

00:04:02.867 --> 00:04:04.702
When he told his family
what he had seen,

00:04:04.786 --> 00:04:06.996
his father concluded
the child had survived

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.123
an encounter with a Tikbalang.

00:04:09.207 --> 00:04:11.876
His mother wisely warned him
to never go into the woods

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.628
with a stranger again.

00:04:13.711 --> 00:04:15.922
Like much of the folklore
of the Philippines,

00:04:16.005 --> 00:04:17.799
written accounts
of this story appeared

00:04:17.882 --> 00:04:20.301
after Spanish colonizers
began to record

00:04:20.385 --> 00:04:23.680
the practices and beliefs
of the Indigenous peoples.

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:26.933
Missionary priests wrote of
widespread fear of the monster,

00:04:27.016 --> 00:04:30.687
a kind of ghost that can
appear as an animal or monster

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.897
and compel them
to do evil deeds.

00:04:32.981 --> 00:04:35.233
But it's likely that the
oral legend of the Tikbalang

00:04:35.316 --> 00:04:36.317
was altered when translated

00:04:36.401 --> 00:04:39.529
and written down
by the Spanish colonizers.

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:41.364
The Tikbalang legend
likely evolved

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:45.034
as a blend of Filipino
traditions and foreign beliefs.

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:47.787
Filipino animism
practices largely revolve

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:50.915
around respect with
veneration paid to ancestors

00:04:50.999 --> 00:04:53.543
and placation sought
from malevolent spirits.

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:55.211
A tricky invisible forest being

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:57.380
is well within
the realm of possibility.

00:04:57.463 --> 00:05:00.258
"The Aswang Project" presents
an addition to this theory==

00:05:00.341 --> 00:05:02.844
the early Hindu presence
in the Philippines.

00:05:02.927 --> 00:05:05.013
Early religious
iconography of Hinduism

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:06.305
found in the Philippines

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:08.725
dating to the 9th
and 14th centuries

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:10.601
proves the religion
had a cultural presence

00:05:10.685 --> 00:05:11.978
on the islands.

00:05:12.061 --> 00:05:14.689
One of the avatars of
the prominent god Vishnu

00:05:14.772 --> 00:05:17.859
appears with the horse head
on a human male body

00:05:17.942 --> 00:05:19.068
ascribing the attributes

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:21.320
of speed and strength
to the deity,

00:05:21.404 --> 00:05:24.615
characteristics that would be
adopted into existing ideas

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:26.325
of the Tikbalang spirit being.

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:28.286
Indigenous Filipino inhabitants

00:05:28.369 --> 00:05:30.496
adapted components
like foreign language,

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:32.165
social practices, and religion

00:05:32.248 --> 00:05:35.084
into their own existing
cultural beliefs.

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:36.669
So, while many
Indigenous people

00:05:36.753 --> 00:05:39.505
seemed to willingly adopt
religions, like Christianity,

00:05:39.589 --> 00:05:40.631
after the Spanish arrived,

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:43.176
many of their older
spiritual practices

00:05:43.259 --> 00:05:44.886
were maintained in secret.

00:05:44.969 --> 00:05:48.056
In 1613, Father Pedro
de San Buena Ventura

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:51.267
described the Tikbalang as
a giant foul-smelling ghost

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.186
or fantasma with wings

00:05:53.269 --> 00:05:56.898
that kidnapped men and
weakened them until they died.

00:05:56.981 --> 00:06:00.735
Increasingly, the Tikbalang
became seen as evil or demonic,

00:06:00.818 --> 00:06:02.445
to use Christian terminology.

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.030
This paralleled
Spain's movement

00:06:04.113 --> 00:06:05.448
towards a religious framework

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:07.992
that had a defined
system of good and evil,

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:09.911
a dichotomy made
gruesomely obvious

00:06:09.994 --> 00:06:13.372
with the witchcraft trials of
the 16th and 17th centuries.

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:15.291
First introduced
by Muslim travelers

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:17.960
in the southern regions
of the Sulu Archipelago

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:20.296
and what is now known
as the Mindanao Island,

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:22.590
it's likely that
the horses of this region

00:06:22.673 --> 00:06:24.467
came from trade
between Sultanates

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.260
during the 14th
and 15th centuries

00:06:26.344 --> 00:06:29.639
and helped inspire the
Tikbalang's horse form.

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:32.308
With the arrival of the
Spanish Conquistadors in 1565,

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:34.936
the horse population
in the Philippines

00:06:35.019 --> 00:06:37.188
grew and became sustainable.

00:06:37.271 --> 00:06:40.316
Spanish colonialism
depended heavily on horses--

00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:42.985
on the military advantage,
travel convenience

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:45.655
and perceived social status
equines provided.

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:47.240
The island location
of the Philippines

00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:48.950
was a significant
traveling distance

00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:50.576
from the Spanish supply lines

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:52.912
of the animals located
in Central America,

00:06:52.995 --> 00:06:55.039
North America, and Europe,

00:06:55.123 --> 00:06:58.126
so they turned to China
and Japan for horses.

00:06:58.209 --> 00:06:59.919
By the beginning of
the 17th century,

00:07:00.002 --> 00:07:01.295
horse trade
and breeding farms

00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:03.297
were well established
in the area.

00:07:03.381 --> 00:07:05.675
A lack of natural pasturage
and food supply

00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:06.801
meant the horses
bred in the islands

00:07:06.884 --> 00:07:08.970
were smaller than
their ancestors.

00:07:09.053 --> 00:07:10.847
Continuing into
the 18th century

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.056
when missionaries uncovered

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.225
Indigenous
spiritual traditions,

00:07:14.308 --> 00:07:16.435
they quickly labeled
the practicing individuals

00:07:16.519 --> 00:07:19.856
as wizards who could
cause or cure illness

00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:22.233
and who could change
themselves into animals

00:07:22.316 --> 00:07:24.026
in order to kill others.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.320
The more frequent references
to horses with Tikbalang lore

00:07:26.404 --> 00:07:29.157
may have resulted
from these changes,

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:32.410
with Spanish text descriptions
increasingly accentuating

00:07:32.493 --> 00:07:34.620
the hybridity of
the monster's appearance.

00:07:34.704 --> 00:07:37.331
Today, this interpretation
of the Tikbalang lingers,

00:07:37.415 --> 00:07:39.292
particularly in comics.

00:07:39.375 --> 00:07:41.961
Arnold Arre's award-winning
"The Mythology Class"

00:07:42.044 --> 00:07:43.921
first published in 1999,

00:07:44.005 --> 00:07:45.548
was really
the first introduction

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:48.134
of the Tikbalang to
modern popular culture.

00:07:48.217 --> 00:07:49.260
Extremely muscular
and tattooed,

00:07:49.343 --> 00:07:52.180
Arre's version
appears more in line

00:07:52.263 --> 00:07:54.056
with what readers
had come to expect

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.225
in traditional
superhero comics.

00:07:56.309 --> 00:07:58.477
Their portrayal changed

00:07:58.561 --> 00:08:01.397
when they started to
appear in pop culture.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.775
That's when the
creators of their times,

00:08:04.859 --> 00:08:08.779
whether it was the '80s, '90s,
or comic-book creators today

00:08:08.863 --> 00:08:10.948
have started to take liberties

00:08:11.032 --> 00:08:13.242
on how to portray
the Tikbalang.

00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:14.493
It's one of those creatures

00:08:14.577 --> 00:08:16.245
that has become
pretty much open

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:19.916
to interpretation as far
as what is the best way

00:08:19.999 --> 00:08:21.250
they serve the story.

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:22.710
(Dr. Zarka)
In the world of "Trese,"

00:08:22.793 --> 00:08:26.130
the creatures are also
incredibly strong and muscular.

00:08:26.214 --> 00:08:29.467
Instead of shape shifting, they
can produce a magical glamor

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.428
that allows them to
appear as regular humans.

00:08:32.511 --> 00:08:33.888
Now a popular Netflix series,

00:08:33.971 --> 00:08:35.640
the animated portrayal
of the Tikbalang

00:08:35.723 --> 00:08:39.685
reinforces the muscular
horse hybrid appearance

00:08:39.769 --> 00:08:42.188
to an even wider
global audience.

00:08:42.271 --> 00:08:45.066
These are our stories,

00:08:45.149 --> 00:08:48.444
and if we don't retell them,
they will soon be forgotten.

00:08:48.527 --> 00:08:52.406
I think we need to keep
retelling these stories

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:56.786
and find new ways to tell them,
tell them in our own ways.

00:08:56.869 --> 00:09:02.541
It's not because we want this
to be the definitive storyline

00:09:02.625 --> 00:09:03.834
of what the Tikbalang is,

00:09:03.918 --> 00:09:07.463
but again, it's what
sparks the imagination.

00:09:07.546 --> 00:09:09.257
It's what strikes fear.

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:12.426
And hopefully, this will
inspire the reader or viewer

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.220
to want to know more.

00:09:14.303 --> 00:09:16.639
(Dr. Zarka)
Whether it's the terrifying
and gangly version

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:18.557
of Tikbalang
from tales told

00:09:18.641 --> 00:09:20.559
in the Philippines
centuries ago,

00:09:20.643 --> 00:09:22.562
or the ripped
and tatted incarnation

00:09:22.645 --> 00:09:25.898
that we see on Netflix today,
one thing is certain:

00:09:25.982 --> 00:09:30.027
The legend is likely to endure
for many years to come.

00:09:30.111 --> 00:09:31.153
Sulu Archipelago.

00:09:31.237 --> 00:09:32.488
Sulu Archipelago.

00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:34.240
The Sulu Archipelago.

00:09:34.323 --> 00:09:35.491
Archip-eh-lago!

00:09:35.574 --> 00:09:36.867
The Sulu Archipelago.

00:09:36.951 --> 00:09:38.619
Archi-pelago.

00:09:38.703 --> 00:09:39.704
Archi-pel-ago.

00:09:39.787 --> 00:09:41.080
Archi-pel-ago.

00:09:41.163 --> 00:09:43.791
Sulu Archipelago. [laughs]

00:09:43.874 --> 00:09:45.793
Of the Sulu Archi-pelay-go.

00:09:45.876 --> 00:09:47.128
I think we just need to be done.

